# CHANGELOG.md is append-only: every PR adds an entry at the same spot under
# [Unreleased], so the first merge forces a rebase on every other open PR.
# The union driver keeps both sides' entries instead of conflicting.
# Deliberately scoped to this one file — union resolves by concatenation with no
# conflict, which is correct for a changelog and wrong for structured files.
#
# CAVEAT — this does NOT stop github.com from showing "This branch has conflicts".
# Merge drivers are a local-checkout feature. GitHub computes PR mergeability
# server-side with the default text driver and does not apply this rule, so a PR
# that touches [Unreleased] flips to CONFLICTING as soon as another such PR
# merges ahead of it. The driver makes the fix free; it does not prevent the flag.
#
# Measured 2026-08-26 (WIC-1363): of 30 open PRs, 25 touched CHANGELOG.md and 17
# read CONFLICTING — 15 of those 17 in both sets. On an 8-PR sample, CHANGELOG.md
# conflicted under the default driver every time, and in 7 of the 8 it was the
# *only* conflicting file, i.e. those PRs were otherwise clean.
#
# So: do not go looking for a real conflict, and do not rebase a PR that others
# are stacked on. Merge main in and push — the driver resolves it on the way
# through, and the merge commit is what flips GitHub back to mergeable:
#
#     git fetch origin && git merge origin/main && git push
#
# If that leaves conflict markers in CHANGELOG.md, the driver did not fire (the
# file is only union-merged when .gitattributes is present in the checkout) and
# you are resolving by hand. Keep BOTH entries, newest date first; the sides are
# additive and neither edits the other's text.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
